From e35a7dd32d34fc752881c22b2ca7c8823d319aa4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 16:41:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(proof): a map of where the work was done, that cannot
 publish an address
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Do you work in my area?" is the second question every visitor has, and until
now this site answered it only with claims. There are 32 service-area pages, and
a service-area page is an assertion: we say we work in Leaside because we wrote
a page saying so. /where-we-work is the other kind of statement — here is a job,
here is the year, here is the square footage, here is the document with the
moisture readings in it.

THE ADDRESSES ARE NOT ON IT, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN

The owner has them. The instinct — "show them the houses" — is a good instinct
pointed at the wrong object, and publishing them would be the one mistake on
this site that cannot be walked back:

  · A map of private homes, each labelled with what was installed and roughly
    what it cost, is a shopping list. "3,800 sq ft of maple and a custom inlay
    staircase, at this pin" is worth more to a burglar than to a customer.
  · Those addresses are personal information of the homeowners under PIPEDA.
    Consent to have a floor done is not consent to be mapped.
  · Scrapers, Street View cross-referencing and the Internet Archive make a
    published address permanent within hours of the first crawl.

And it buys nothing. Nobody picks a contractor because a pin sits on a specific
driveway; they pick one because the work is demonstrably nearby and demonstrably
real. Neighbourhood precision delivers all of the persuasion and none of the
exposure — and it is what local search actually rewards, because relevance is
computed over areas and entities, not over street numbers.

scripts/verify-work-map.mjs MAKES THAT STRUCTURAL

Six checks, so it is not a matter of anyone remembering:

  1. NO ADDRESS, BY SHAPE. No Canadian postal code, no street address, no unit
     number, and no field whose NAME contains address/street/postal/unit/suite/
     civic — matched as a case-insensitive substring, so `streetAddress:` and
     `civicNumber:` are the same mistake wearing camelCase.
  2. THREE DECIMALS, NOT FOUR. 3 dp is ~110 m: a neighbourhood. 4 dp is ~11 m:
     a house. One keystroke apart, so the keystroke is banned rather than
     trusted.
  3. INSIDE THE FRAME. Catches transposed lat/lng — the failure mode where wrong
     data still looks plausible, because it is only a dot slightly elsewhere.
  4. EVERY PIN IS EVIDENCE. Each entry names a case study, and the guard re-reads
     that .mdx and compares neighbourhood, year and square footage. A pin cannot
     be typed in; it is earned by publishing the job. Same contract as
     verify-job-cards.mjs, and it means the map can never be padded.
  5. THE AREA AND SERVICE ARE REAL PAGES, so every pin links somewhere.
  6. UNVERIFIED COORDINATES DO NOT REACH SCHEMA. COORDS_VERIFIED is false, so no
     GeoCoordinates node may be emitted. A 29 km-wide drawing tolerates a 300 m
     error; a machine-readable claim does not, because nothing downstream will
     ever re-check it. Runbook §11 is the 20-minute task that flips it.

Six negative tests. The FIRST one failed: I put "18 Chestnut Park Road" in a
`streetAddress` field and the guard passed it — the field pattern demanded an
exact token, and the street pattern allowed only one word before "Road", which
misses most streets in this city. Both fixed, both re-tested. A privacy guard
that passes the thing it exists to stop is worse than none, because it is
trusted.

THE PICTURE

Inline SVG. No tile vendor — a basemap is not a picture, it is a stream of
requests handing every visitor's IP and viewport to a third party, which makes
that vendor a declarable data processor on /privacy and a new CSP origin. P2.4
was spent REMOVING a third party that was declared and never even fired; adding
one back to draw five dots would be the same mistake with a nicer view. Two
kilobytes, zero requests, renders with JavaScript off.

Equirectangular with the longitude axis scaled by cos(latitude): at 43.7°N a
degree of longitude is 80 km against 111, so without it the city comes out ~40%
too wide and Etobicoke lands further from downtown than Newmarket. Two lines,
and the difference between a map and a diagram.

The frame fits the pins, with a FLOOR of ~13 km on how far it may zoom. Fitting
alone would have zoomed five clustered downtown jobs until the frame was a few
hundred metres across and the dots started to look like addresses — undoing the
privacy design in a rendering detail. The list underneath is the accessible
representation and carries the facts; the SVG is aria-hidden, because a scatter
of circles read aloud is noise.

Five pins today, because five jobs are published with their measurements. The
map gets denser the way everything else here does: by publishing the next job.

Also: runbook §12 on advertising sequencing — why the Meta pixel is a legal-
surface change and not a script tag, and why the first paid dollar belongs on
search intent with server-side conversions, not on cold social.

VERIFY: 46 guards green. 36 public routes, 0 orphans. tsc adds nothing.

